Chelsea, as a [[Premier League]] team involved in the [[2021–22 UEFA Champions League]], started in the Third Round. Their first match was at home to fellow Premier League side [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]]. [[Timo Werner]] put Chelsea ahead in the second half, only for [[Cameron Archer]] to equalise for Villa. The match went to a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]], which Chelsea won 4–3.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chelsea 1–1 Aston Villa (4–3 on pens): Blues win shootout to reach Carabao Cup last 16 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/58566909 |website=BBC Sport |access-date=12 January 2022}}</ref> In the next round, they were again given a home draw against a Premier League side, this time [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]]. Chelsea again took the lead through [[Kai Havertz]] just before half-time, but [[Ché Adams]] equalised just after the interval to again take the tie to a penalty shoot-out, which Chelsea again won 4–3.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/58963258 |title=Chelsea 1–1 Southampton (4–3 pens): Chelsea edge Southampton to reach Carabao Cup quarter-finals |website=BBC Sport |access-date=12 January 2022}}</ref> In the quarter-finals, Chelsea received their first away draw, coming up against [[West London derby|West London rivals]] and fellow Premier League side [[Brentford F.C.|Brentford]]. The match went goalless until 10 minutes from the end, when Brentford's [[Pontus Jansson]] scored an [[own goal]], before [[Jorginho (footballer, born December 1991)|Jorginho]] sealed a 2–0 win for Chelsea with a penalty.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/59662104 |title=Brentford 0–2 Chelsea: Blues overcome neighbours to reach Carabao Cup semis |website=BBC Sport |access-date=12 January 2022}}</ref> In the two-legged semi-final, Chelsea drew yet another Premier League side in [[Chelsea F.C.–Tottenham Hotspur F.C. rivalry|London rivals]] [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]]. In the first leg at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]], Chelsea won 2–0; both goals came in the first half, first from Havertz before Tottenham's [[Ben Davies (footballer, born 1993)|Ben Davies]] scored an own goal.<ref>{{cite news |first=Emlyn |last=Begley |title=Chelsea 2-0 Tottenham Hotspur: Blues win Carabao Cup semi-final first leg |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/59814563 |website=BBC Sport |date=5 January 2022 |access-date=12 January 2022 }}</ref> In the return leg at [[Tottenham Hotspur Stadium]], a 1–0 win thanks to a goal from [[Antonio Rüdiger]] was enough to put Chelsea through to the final 3–0 on aggregate.<ref>{{Cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Chelsea beat Spurs to reach EFL Cup final |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/59876396 |website=BBC Sport |date=12 January 2022 |access-date=13 January 2022 }}</ref>

